INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

A wood stove, a kerosene lamp, a table scarred by decades of use. Snow ticks at the window.

OTTILIE, sixty, in a wool cardigan, stirs a pot. Her son RASMUS, thirty-five, a duffel bag still on his shoulder, stands in the doorway with snow on his boots.

RASMUS
Mama.

OTTILIE
You didn't write.

RASMUS
I wrote, Mama. Three times.

OTTILIE
Not one letter came.

RASMUS
Then the post is broken.

She keeps stirring. He steps in, sets the bag down.

OTTILIE
Wash your hands. There's soup.

RASMUS
I'm not hungry.

OTTILIE
You're thin.

RASMUS
I'm the same as I was.

OTTILIE
You were never this thin, Rasmus.

He sits. She ladles soup into a bowl, sets it before him, and sits across the table. Neither touches a spoon.

RASMUS
I sold the boat.

OTTILIE
I know.

RASMUS
Who told you?

OTTILIE
Hennie at the church. Her husband bought the nets.

RASMUS
Mama, I had to.

OTTILIE
Your father built that boat.

RASMUS
And it was sinking us, Mama. Debts. Three years of debts.

OTTILIE
You could have asked.

RASMUS
Asked who? You? With what?

OTTILIE
(rising)
With a little respect, Rasmus!

RASMUS
I'm trying, Mama!

OTTILIE
You sold your father's boat and you come home thin and you don't write!

RASMUS
I wrote!

She strikes the table with the flat of her hand. The lamp jumps. Silence, heavy and total.

RASMUS (CONT'D)
(quietly)
I wrote, Mama. I wrote every week. I think someone has been keeping them from you.

OTTILIE
Who?

RASMUS
Who collects the post?

Ottilie goes pale. She turns to the dresser and opens a drawer. In it, a bundle of envelopes tied with twine.

She holds them a long moment. Then she places them on the table between them.

OTTILIE
I could not read them. My eyes. I was ashamed.

RASMUS
Oh, Mama.

He reaches across and covers her hand with his. The soup steams between them, ignored.
